   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to authorizing\nretail licensees to purchase alcohol with a business payment card",
    "basePrintNo": "S8855",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8855-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 5,
       "fullName": "Jim Gaughran",
       "imgName": "1042_Jim_Gaughran.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1222,
       "sessionMemberId": 1807,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "GAUGHRAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §101-aa, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8855\n\nSPONSOR: KAMINSKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nauthorizing retail licensees to purchase alcohol with a business payment\ncard\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo expand the payment options available to retail licensees of spirits\nto pay manufacturers and wholesalers to include business payment cards.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends section 101-aa of the alcoholic beverage\ncontrol law to add the definitions of \"business payment card,\" \"final\ncash invoice amount,\" and \"final business payment card invoice amount.\"\n\nSection two amends section 101-aa to provide retail licensees the addi-\ntional payment option of a business payment card in transactions with\nmanufacturers and wholesalers of spirits. This section additionally\nprovides that manufacturers and wholesalers that exercise reasonable\ndiligence in ensuring the sale comports with the law shall not be found\nto have violated the subdivision where a retail licensee uses a credit\ncard other than a business payment card.\n\nSection three amends section 101-aa to provide that a manufacturer or\nwholesaler that accepts business payment cards shall clearly state the\nfinal cash invoice amount and the final business payment card invoice\namount on an invoice provided to the retail licensee.\n\nSection four provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, retail licensees have the ability to purchase beer and wine\nproducts for resale and on or off premises consumption with a business\npayment card, which is a credit or debit card issued to the licensee for\nbusiness or commercial use. This bill will provide the same option for\nthe purchase and sale of spirits by mirroring the existing law as it\napplies to beer and wine products. This bill also provides that manufac-\nturers and wholesalers that exercise reasonable diligence shall not be\nfound to be in violation of the law where a retail licensee uses a cred-\nit card other than a business payment card. Finally, it provides that\nthe law shall not be construed to require such parties to accept busi-\nness payment cards as a method of payment.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATION:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
